.TH CHMAP_CREATE_FULL 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_create_full \- create a hash map with full customization
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

typedef unsigned long (*ccol_hashing_proc_t)(const void *ptr, size_t size);
typedef bool (*ccol_key_equality_proc_t)(const void *first,
                                         size_t first_size,
                                         const void *second,
                                         size_t second_size);

chmap chmap_create_full(size_t initial_bucket_array_size,
                        ccol_data_type key_type,
                        ccol_data_type val_type,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        ccol_hashing_proc_t custom_hashing_proc,
                        ccol_key_equality_proc_t custom_key_equality_proc,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a hash map with full control over the initial size, the types,
memory management, the hash function and the key equality function. The
map selects its implementation strategy automatically from the key and
value types and from custom_key_equality_proc.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
initial_bucket_array_size  Initial number of buckets
key_type                   Type of keys
val_type                   Type of values
mmgmt_procs                Custom memory management, or NULL
custom_hashing_proc        Custom hash function, or NULL
custom_key_equality_proc   Custom key equality function, or NULL for the
                           built-in comparison; it needs a
                           custom_hashing_proc that agrees with it
err                        Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the newly created hash map on success.
Returns NULL on failure, for example when a custom_key_equality_proc is
given without a custom_hashing_proc.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM KEY EQUALITY"
.PP
A key equality procedure receives both keys and both sizes, and returns true
when the two are the same key. The map uses it in place of its built-in
comparison for every lookup, update and delete. It must agree with
custom_hashing_proc: two keys that it reports as equal must get the same
hash. The map refuses the procedure without a custom hash, because the
built-in hash reads the bytes of a key and so cannot agree with an equality
that treats two different byte images as one key. The pointers carry no
alignment for the key type, so read each field through memcpy.
.PP
A map with a key equality procedure always uses separate chaining, whatever
the key and value types are.
.SH "KEYS WITH PADDING"
.PP
Without a key equality procedure, a key of a type that the map does not know
(ccol_other_types, such as a struct) equals another key only when every
byte of the two is equal, and the default hash also reads every byte,
padding bytes of a struct included. Two keys with equal members but
different padding are then two different keys, and a lookup misses.
.PP
Give such a key type a custom_hashing_proc and a custom_key_equality_proc
that read only the members. Without them, the bytes of a padded key must be
fully determined. Through the raw functions, such as chmap_insert_elem(3),
the map reads exactly the object that key_pair->ptr points at, so zero the
whole key object with memset() before you set its members. Through the
type-inferred macros, the map sees the macro's own copy of the key, and C
leaves the padding of a struct copy unspecified. With GCC 11 or later the
macros set that padding to zero; with any other compiler, a padded key
passed through the macros needs the two procedures.
.SH "HASHING AND RESISTANCE TO HASH FLOODING"
.PP
Every map has two hash modes and starts in the fast mode. In the fast
mode, an integer, a float, a double and a pointer key get a Fibonacci
multiply: the key times the odd 64-bit constant nearest to 2^64 divided by
the golden ratio, read from the high bits of the product. A 64-bit key is
multiplied at 64 bits on a 32-bit target as well, so that both of its halves
reach the index. A key with no fixed width, such as a string (ccol_string)
or a struct (ccol_other_types), gets XXH64 with a secret 64-bit seed, and the
result of a custom_hashing_proc goes through the murmur3 finalizer. In this
mode a sequential run of integers costs one probe for each lookup, while
integers scaled by a power of two, aligned addresses, packed pairs, paths,
identifiers and field names spread as a random function would.
.PP
In the keyed mode, a fixed-width key and the result of a
custom_hashing_proc go through a mixer of two multiplies that starts from
an exclusive or with a secret 64-bit seed, and a key with no fixed width
gets SipHash-1-3 with a secret 128-bit key. SipHash-1-3 is a keyed
pseudorandom function: no two inputs collide for every key, and without
the key two inputs collide only by chance. The mixer spreads every set of
keys as a random function would, so a lookup costs about 1.3 probes at a
load of 0.38 and about 2 at the maximum load of 0.70. A long double key
gets SipHash-1-3 by its value in both modes. The keys that the map treats
as the same key, -0.0 and 0.0 included, are the same in both modes.
.PP
The fast hashes are either public or keyed in a way that some sets of keys
defeat: XXH64 has collisions that hold for every seed. A party that
controls the keys, for example the member names of a JSON document or the
identifiers in a request that arrives from the network, can therefore
choose a set that falls into one bucket or one cluster of a fast map. To
detect this, every insert of a new key measures how far it had to go: the
displacement of its slot from its home slot, or the number of nodes in the
chain that it joins. A map switches to the keyed mode when:
.IP \(bu 2
one open-addressing insert lands more than its cap from its home slot (the
cap is 24 slots for each doubling of the table up to 4096 slots, and 288
slots from there on);
.IP \(bu 2
one separate-chaining insert meets 20 nodes;
.IP \(bu 2
256 consecutive inserts of new keys cost more in total than 256 times
5 * E(a) + 0.5 slots in an open-addressing table of 4096 slots or more,
6 * E(a) + 0.5 in a smaller one, or 4.5 * lambda + 0.5 nodes under
separate chaining. Here E(a) = (1 / (1 - a)^2 - 1) / 2 is the expected
displacement of an insert at load a under a random function, and lambda is
the number of entries for each bucket. Both a and lambda are the highest
load that the 256 inserts can have reached, so a bulk delete among them
does not lower the bound;
.IP \(bu 2
a shrink merges clusters or chains past the first two limits.
.PP
A lookup never writes to the map and never switches it. Until a map
switches, a party that controls the keys can make an open-addressing insert
cost about 26 probes on average at a load of 0.70 (about 31 in a table
below 4096 slots), and a single insert no more than the cap; a
separate-chaining insert meets about 7.3 nodes on average at 1.5 entries
for each bucket, and always fewer than 20. A lookup or a delete in an
open-addressing map examines at most one slot more than the largest
displacement of any key in the table, which the cap bounds while the map is
fast. This also bounds a lookup inside a long run of keys whose home slots
are consecutive, a run that no insert has to pay for.
.PP
The caps sit far above what ordinary keys produce. Under a random function,
an open-addressing insert at the highest load is displaced by more than 288
slots about once in 1.4 * 10^9 inserts, and a separate-chaining insert at
1.5 entries for each bucket meets 20 nodes about once in 10^15.
.PP
The switch rebuilds an open-addressing table at the same size, and the
references that the map handed out become invalid, as they do at any
insert. When that allocation fails, the map stays correct and in the fast
mode, the insert that asked for the switch still succeeds, and the next
insert that asks for it tries again. A separate-chaining map rehashes its
nodes in place and allocates nothing, so every reference to a key or a
value, the insertion order and every iterator stay valid. A reset and a
shrink keep the mode.
.PP
When a keyed map grows, it tries the fast mode on the new table (the
doubled table of an open-addressing map, or the bucket array of a
separate-chaining map that grows by four). The map keeps the fast mode
unless that fill shows that the keys collide under it: an open-addressing
entry that would land more than the cap of the new table from its home
slot, a separate-chaining chain that would reach 20 nodes, or displacements
or chain walks that add up to more than the count plus 64. The attempt
stops at the first of these, and the growth then fills the same new table
with the keyed hash, so it allocates nothing more. For a map of n entries,
a failed attempt inspects at most 2 * n + cap + 65 slots, or hashes n nodes
and walks at most n + 83 chain nodes, in addition to the keyed fill.
Because growth is geometric, a map whose every growth fails pays a constant
amount of extra work for each insert: in an open-addressing table of 4096
slots or more, at most about four slots and two keyed placements for each
insert, and under separate chaining about three node operations and 1.3
keyed hashes. A map that returns to the fast mode is judged by its inserts
again, so an attack that resumes switches it within two windows of 256
inserts. Integer sets that cluster under the multiply only at some sizes,
such as multiples of 24, 32, 48 or 96, switch at a small size and come back
to the fast mode at a later growth whose table spreads them well; at other
sizes they cluster again and the window switches the map back. Whether it
succeeds or not, the attempt keeps every entry, and under separate chaining
it keeps every reference to a key or a value, the insertion order and every
iterator. When the allocation of a growth fails, the map keeps its table
and its mode, as any growth does.
.PP
The library draws the secrets from getrandom(2) once for each process, the
first time that the process creates a map. When the kernel cannot answer
without blocking, the library mixes the clocks, the process id and
randomized addresses instead. Every map of the process uses the same
secrets for its whole life, and a child made by fork(2) keeps the secrets
of its parent. The iteration order of a separate-chaining map follows
insertion and does not depend on the mode or on the secrets. An
open-addressing map iterates in slot order, so its order depends on the
mode, and in the keyed mode it differs from one process to the next; see
chashmap_begin_iter(3).
.PP
Two keys to which a custom_hashing_proc gives the same value collide in
both modes, because no finalizer can tell them apart. A map whose keys come
from an untrusted party through a custom hash therefore needs a custom
function that two such keys cannot share.
.SH NOTES
.PP
A custom hash does not have to spread its own entropy. The map derives a
bucket or slot index from the high bits of a hash, and it first passes a
value that comes from ccol_hashing_proc_t through the finalizer of the mode
of the map. A hash can be an identity, a counter, or a value already reduced
modulo a small number, and it still distributes well.
.nf
Implementation selection:
  - Open addressing: both key and value are integral types <=8 bytes,
    and custom_key_equality_proc is NULL
  - Separate chaining: either key or value is non-integral or >8 bytes,
    or custom_key_equality_proc is not NULL
Integral types: char, short, int, long, long long (signed/unsigned),
               float, double
long double is excluded from open addressing (it can be 10-16 bytes).
Open addressing doubles the table on an insert of a new key once the
live entries fill more than 0.70 of it, and halves it on a delete that
leaves less than 0.25 of it live. A delete moves the later entries of
its cluster back into the gap, so the table holds no marker for a
deleted slot and only live entries decide a resize. A grow leaves the
live load between 0.25 and 0.50, and a shrink leaves it below 0.50.
For a float or double key_type, the map always canonicalizes a -0.0
key to 0.0 before it hashes, stores or compares the key, which matches
-0.0 == 0.0 in C. This is always true, also when custom_hashing_proc
is not NULL: the custom function always gets the already-canonicalized
bit pattern, and the map stores that canonicalized value.
For a long double key_type, the map always uses separate chaining, and
key equality and the hash use the numeric value of the key instead of
its raw bytes. The keys -0.0L and 0.0L collide into one key, as -0.0
and 0.0 do for float and double, and two keys that hold the same value
stay the same key even when their platform-defined padding bits are
different. Every NaN long double collapses into one key, unlike float
and double, where each different NaN payload is a different key.
The map does not use this value-based handling when
custom_hashing_proc is not NULL: the custom function then gets the
raw bytes, padding included. A custom hash function for a long double
key type must therefore itself use the numeric value, for example by
hashing the result of frexpl(3); otherwise a lookup can miss a key
that is in the map.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { char tag; long id; } tagged_id;  /* padding after tag */

unsigned long tagged_id_hash(const void *p, size_t size) {
    tagged_id k;
    (void)size;
    memcpy(&k, p, sizeof(k));
    return (unsigned long)k.id * 31ul + (unsigned char)k.tag;
}

bool tagged_id_eq(const void *a, size_t as, const void *b, size_t bs) {
    tagged_id x, y;
    (void)as; (void)bs;
    memcpy(&x, a, sizeof(x));
    memcpy(&y, b, sizeof(y));
    return x.tag == y.tag && x.id == y.id;
}

char *err = NULL;
chmap m = chmap_create_full(16, ccol_other_types, ccol_int, NULL,
                            tagged_id_hash, tagged_id_eq, &err);
if (!m) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot create map: %s\en", err);
    return 1;
}
/* ... chmap_insert_elem(m, ...) ... */
chmap_destroy(m);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
